    modelAnswer: '(a) Buyers cannot judge unknown shops; Amazon’s reputation solves this in the US, but Klarna already does so in Sweden, so Amazon’s advantage is smaller. (b) One price is a compromise: Thai consumers pay more and may not be served, Swedish consumers pay less; total welfare is ambiguous. (c) Yes: second-degree discrimination, players self-select between paying with attention to ads and buying items. (d) MR = 20 − 2Q = 2 gives Q = 9, p = 11, profit 81. (e) With free songs the consumer plays 20; the fee equals her surplus, 200; profit is 200 − 40 = 160. The fee captures all consumer surplus, whereas a single price leaves surplus and sells too few songs; free usage, however, leads to songs worth less than their cost.',
